<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>WinExe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0-windows</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <UseWindowsForms>true</UseWindowsForms>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <!-- 2026-08-06: renamed the RUNNING PROCESS only (AssemblyName -> compiled exe name), per
         explicit user request — everything else (namespace, ProgramData folder, scheduled task
         names, window/tray text) deliberately stays "SentinelRemoteAssist". Every hardcoded
         "SentinelRemoteAssist.exe" filename reference across this codebase (WatchdogRunner's
         process match, EdrWatcher/NukeRunner's own-tooling exclusion lists, AutoStartManager,
         SilentInstaller, the support service's embedded PowerShell) was updated in the same change —
         see git history. -->
    <AssemblyName>Sentinel</AssemblyName>
    <ApplicationManifest>app.manifest</ApplicationManifest>
    <ApplicationIcon Condition="Exists('app.ico')">app.ico</ApplicationIcon>

    <!-- Single source of truth for the agent version — drives AssemblyVersion/FileVersion/
         InformationalVersion (SDK default derivation), which Config.AgentVersion reads back via
         FileVersionInfo at runtime, and which the bastion WebUI reads remotely over Hop-2
         (`(Get-Item ...).VersionInfo.FileVersion`) to show what build is actually running on a
         customer's box. Bump on any meaningful client behavior change (mirrors the Android APK's
         versionName bump convention in this repo). 1.1.0 = first tracked version + RestartOnFailure
         task setting; 1.1.1 = watchdog self-heal when AutoConnect=1 (RestartOnFailure alone does
         not re-kick a Start-ScheduledTask-started tunnel after force-kill — proved 2026-07-11);
         1.1.2 = version bump only, validated the fully-remote update pipeline against the fixed
         create_session() (never kills a connected session on remint) — remint-while-connected
         confirmed safe, but remote-update-session.sh itself had two bugs found in that same run
         (WRITER's $ErrorActionPreference='Stop' turned schtasks' harmless "task doesn't exist yet"
         stderr into a terminating error; a nested f-string in the final version-check broke Python
         parsing) — both fixed. 1.1.3 = version bump only, validates the corrected script end-to-end
         with remint included (not the no-remint path). 1.2.0 = Endpoint Application Control (WDAC
         default-deny) support: a new appcontrol-watch mode (AppControlWatcher.cs) tails
         Microsoft-Windows-CodeIntegrity/Operational and reports blocked/audit events to the
         bastion; TunnelRunner.cs gains a conditional local forward (endpoint-config.json-gated) so
         the watcher can reach the bastion's WebUI API over the existing tunnel. 1.2.1 = fix
         AppControlWatcher reporting the raw NT device path (\Device\HarddiskVolumeN\...) instead
         of a drive-letter path — broke Approve's Test-Path file-identity check (found live M5:
         "file no longer present" even though the file was right there). Now resolved via
         QueryDosDevice before the report is sent. 1.3.1 = two unattended-mode fixes found live
         2026-07-15: (a) UAC popped on EVERY dashboard session start until a reboot — the
         interactive UI process holds the control channel before a reboot, so it (not the SYSTEM
         process) handled each remote_assist_ssh_key push, and RunElevatedSetup() gated its no-UAC
         scheduled-task path on IsUnattendedProcess alone → always `runas`. Now routes through the
         registered SYSTEM AutoSetup task whenever it exists, from either process; also hoisted the
         ExtraPubKeyPath persist so the interactive `runas` path actually installs the freshly
         pushed Hop-2 key. (b) "Start automatically" stopped working after a reboot — the
         unattended-tunnel process's finally called ClearEnrolled(), deleting the persistent
         enrollment marker that the next boot's AtStartup tunnel needs; removed. 1.3.2 = fixes the
         REAL cause of 1.3.1's two symptoms, found live 2026-07-15 on the QEMU VM: in autostart mode
         the interactive UI window took the single control-channel mutex and won, but then deferred
         Hop-1 to the SYSTEM background service, which was blocked forever waiting on that same mutex
         — so dashboard sessions stayed "Orphan" (never connected) AND every session start re-popped
         UAC (the non-elevated UI handled the key install via runas; the scheduled-task workaround
         it fell back to never actually executes when triggered on demand). Fix: (a) MainForm — in
         autostart mode the interactive UI is status-only, it hands the control channel to the
         SYSTEM service (ReleaseControlChannelInteractive) instead of holding it; (b) SetupRunner —
         the SYSTEM service runs setup in-process (already elevated, live pushed key, no UAC, no
         flaky task) instead of the scheduled-task hop. 1.3.5 = control-channel watchdog: found
         live 2026-07-17 on the QEMU VM that a reconnect rejected server-side ("Socket error:
         Invalid signature" — plausibly transient clock skew on a heavily-loaded host) left the
         control channel permanently disconnected with xray/REALITY itself still healthy, so the
         existing CheckXrayHealth() 30s timer (which only ever inspected xray transport health,
         never the Socket.IO connection itself) never noticed — only a full process kill/restart
         recovered it. Same timer now also force-reconnects if the control channel is still down
         ControlChannelStuckThreshold (45s) after the last Connect() attempt, independent of xray's
         own state; also started from Connect() itself (not only inside OnConnected) so it runs
         even if a connection attempt never succeeds at all. Also grows the account-login dialog's
         "Two-factor code" label/row further (22px->26px label, 60px->64px row) and the error row
         (24px->28px) — reported still clipped even after the prior 18px->22px fix for the same
         label; not visually re-verified against a live Windows runtime this round. 1.3.6 = per-
         device Hop-1 identity (new Hop1Identity.cs): replaces the old account-linked flow's ONE
         shared keypair per SentinelMDM account (baked in at build time, RemoteAssistHop1Key.cs,
         now removed) with a keypair each device generates for itself on first run and reports to
         the server on every "register" (hop1PublicKey). Found live 2026-07-18 testing two devices
         under one account for the first time: the shared key meant sshd's authorized_keys matching
         could only ever honor the FIRST device's line, so every OTHER device's own -R port-forward
         was silently denied ("remote port forwarding failed" / server-side "the request was
         denied") no matter how correctly the bastion had provisioned its own port — see docs/ARCHITECTURE.md. New AccountLinkedBuild MSBuild property/compile
         constant replaces RemoteAssistHop1Key.IsConfigured as the "is this a real account-linked
         build" signal now that nothing account-specific is embedded at build time. 1.4.0 = EDR
         Phase 1 (continuous behavioral telemetry): new edr-watch mode (EdrWatcher.cs, mirrors
         AppControlWatcher.cs's shape) tails Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon/Operational (Sysmon deployed
         by the bastion's provisioning script, same pattern as WDAC) and reports process-lineage
         (Office/script-host spawning a shell, or any -enc/-EncodedCommand powershell invocation),
         LSASS ProcessAccess (credential-dump indicator), and CreateRemoteThread (hollowing-adjacent,
         report-only) rule hits to the bastion over TunnelRunner's conditional -L forward. New
         EdrConfig.cs/Config.EdrDir; TunnelRunner.cs's -L forward selection extended from a 2-way to
         a 3-way check (AppControl/WinUpdate/Edr all share one provisioned config/port). Report
         payload field-name mapping cross-checked against Sysmon's documented schema but NOT yet
         independently verified against a real live event on a real Windows box (same caveat
         AppControlWatcher.cs's own first release carried for CodeIntegrity events) — verify on the
         next real test run. 1.4.1 = EDR live-verified end to end 2026-07-19 (real Sysmon events,
         correct decode, correct routing) — found and fixed two real bugs from that live run: (a)
         EdrWatcher.cs now excludes SentinelRemoteAssist's own tooling (sshd-session.exe/sshd.exe/
         ssh-agent.exe/SentinelRemoteAssist.exe/xray.exe under the SentinelRemoteAssist install path)
         as a source/parent image, since every existing bastion-driven PowerShell action (Telemetry,
         WinUpdate, AppLocker, ARP scan, IT audit, EDR's own provisioning) is spawned as a child of
         our own sshd and the -EncodedCommand ones would otherwise ALWAYS match the lineage rule; (b)
         bastion-side enable_edr() now force-reconnects the Hop-1 tunnel immediately after a
         successful enable, since TunnelRunner.cs's -L forward is only decided at connection-BUILD
         time — an already-running tunnel never picks up a newly-written endpoint-config.json on its
         own, so EdrWatcher ran and queued real events to its local outbox but nothing reached
         bastion until the tunnel happened to reconnect. 1.5.0 = EDR Phase 2 (Active Remediation):
         local Kill/Suspend/Resume primitives (Win32Native.cs — thread suspend/resume has no
         managed .NET wrapper — + ProcessIntervention.cs) for the future autonomous-response path;
         a new LocalIsolation.cs shells netsh.exe directly to isolate/restore this host's network
         with zero Hop-2 dependency (writes the same isolation-block.json marker the bastion-side
         dashboard-triggered PS1 pair reads/writes, so status is consistent regardless of which
         path triggered it) — not yet called from anywhere; Phase 3's canary-file/mass-encryption
         detectors are the actual callers. Dashboard-triggered process list/kill, finding-scoped
         file delete, and host isolation block/unblock all landed bastion-side (new PS1 scripts,
         no agent-side change needed for those — they run over the existing arbitrary-Hop-2-script
         primitive, same as every other one-shot dashboard feature). 1.6.0 = EDR Phase 3
         (anti-ransomware + canary files): new RansomwareGuard.cs, started from EdrWatcher.Run()
         alongside the Sysmon watcher (same process/config, not a separate mode). Provisions 3 to 5
         realistically-named decoy files (never overwriting a real file — hard collision check
         first) into each C:\Users\* profile's Documents/Desktop/Pictures/Downloads, watched via
         FileSystemWatcher; ANY touch (change/rename/delete) is autonomous and instant, no toggle —
         kill the attributed process tree (attribution via a new Sysmon Event 11 FileCreate
         correlation buffer in EdrWatcher.cs, TryGetRecentFileTouch) plus local network isolation
         (LocalIsolation.cs from 1.5.0) plus an on-screen notification to the interactive session
         (new SecurityNotification.cs, WTSSendMessage over new Win32Native.cs P/Invokes, since
         edr-watch runs as a SYSTEM/Session-0 task with no desktop of its own to pop a MessageBox
         into). Also a weighted sliding-window mass-file-modification rate heuristic across all
         watched roots (renames with an extension change weighted highest — the classic encryptor
         rename-over-original fingerprint) with an allowlist for known-legitimate bulk-file
         processes (OneDrive/Dropbox/Search Indexer/Defender/backup) — deliberately report-only
         until thresholds are tuned against a real controlled test, per the earlier decision not to
         repeat the Telemetry-v1 ship-untested mistake. A 60s backstop re-checks canary
         existence/content hash in case FileSystemWatcher drops an event during the exact burst
         that matters most. Both new rule types (canary_file_touch, mass_file_modification) added to
         the support service's ingest_edr_report() rule allow-list — NOT yet independently verified
         against a real controlled-detonation run on a Windows box; verify on the next test pass
         before trusting the autonomous kill path in the field. Live-verified 2026-07-19 on the
         canary path specifically (touch -> instant isolate, kill correctly skipped when no
         process was attributed) — mass-modification heuristic still unverified. 1.7.0 = EDR
         Phase 4 (forensic depth): (a) lineage/network context — a new BuildLineageContext()/
         BuildLineageContextByPid() in EdrWatcher.cs surfaces the Phase-1 per-ProcessGuid
         correlation buffer (parent chain + observed network connections) as a `lineage_context`
         field riding along on lsass_access, create_remote_thread, canary_file_touch, and
         mass_file_modification reports — previously that buffer only ever fed the process_lineage
         rule's own report. RecentFileTouches (Phase 3's file-touch attribution buffer) now also
         carries the touching process's ProcessGuid, not just Image/Pid, via Sysmon Event 11's own
         ProcessGuid field. (b) HollowingWatcher.cs — a new, independent, VirtualQueryEx-based
         heuristic (new Win32Native.cs P/Invokes: OpenProcess/VirtualQueryEx/
         MEMORY_BASIC_INFORMATION) checking every running process every 90s for whether its own
         main-module base address is still MEM_IMAGE-backed (the classic unmap+rewrite/RunPE
         hollowing technique replaces that with MEM_PRIVATE) or RWX at that base — narrowly targets
         ONE hollowing technique, NOT process injection in general, and has NOT been validated
         against a real hollowing PoC (only that the P/Invoke plumbing runs cleanly against
         ordinary processes without throwing) — report-only, MEDIUM, one alert per process
         lifetime, never autonomous, same posture as CreateRemoteThread. New rule
         process_hollowing_indicator added to the support service's ingest_edr_report() allow-list. (c)
         Thread-suspend/resume dashboard exposure — new _EDR_PROCESS_SUSPEND_PS1/
         _EDR_PROCESS_RESUME_PS1 in the support service (inline C# Add-Type mirroring
         ProcessIntervention.cs's own OpenThread/SuspendThread/ResumeThread pattern, since Windows
         has no built-in cmdlet for this either), new Suspend/Resume buttons next to Kill in the
         dashboard's EDR process list — freezes/unfreezes every thread of a PID as a lighter-weight
         alternative to Kill. USN Journal (evaluated as the plan's own explicit event-drop
         contingency, not implemented) — no field evidence yet of FileSystemWatcher dropping
         events in practice, so deferred until real Phase 3 usage shows it's actually needed.
         Live-verified 2026-07-19 against a real deployed 1.7.0.0: found and fixed a real bug
         where Kill/Suspend/Resume's PS1 param name $Pid collided with PowerShell's read-only
         automatic $PID variable (the support service only, no agent-side change — see that repo's own
         history). 1.7.1 = RansomwareGuard.cs's autonomous-action storm-guard cap raised 5 -> 500
         per 2-minute window, explicit user call ("make it high enough it won't miss killing
         anything") — 5 was too conservative against the realistic worst case of many canaries
         (up to 5 names x 4 folders x N real user profiles) being hit close together during a fast,
         non-selective ransomware sweep; 500 comfortably exceeds any plausible on-box canary count
         while still bounding a genuinely pathological infinite-loop bug from hammering
         taskkill/netsh forever. No other behavior change. 1.8.0 = S3 Bucket Network Drive
         (S3DriveManager.cs): mounts two S3 prefixes as real local folders (Private + Public),
         each `subst`-mapped to its own drive letter (prefers Z:, falls back to the next free
         letter independently per share), kept in sync via `rclone bisync` on a 3-minute timer.
         Encrypted with rclone's `crypt` remote, connection-string-chained over an on-the-fly S3
         remote (no rclone.conf ever written to disk). Public share password is account-wide
         (every device the account controls shares one password + the same `public/` prefix so
         they can collaborate); Private share password is set per-device on that device's own
         WebUI page and never shared. Config/secrets arrive over the existing RA Socket.IO control
         channel (`set_s3_drive` command, mirrors set_vpn) — rclone then talks to S3 directly from
         this box, never routed through the bastion or server. Drive-letter visibility across the
         SYSTEM-vs-interactive execution split is handled via a per-interactive-user AtLogOn
         scheduled task (discovering the logged-in username via Win32_ComputerSystem.UserName,
         same SYSTEM->interactive-session bridging technique as the screenshot-over-SSH procedure
         documented in docs/ARCHITECTURE.md). Config persisted DPAPI-protected (LocalMachine scope, same
         treatment as DeviceIdentity.cs's own key store) rather than VpnManager's plaintext-JSON
         precedent, since this file holds a real S3 secret key + obscured share passwords. 1.8.1 =
         three fixes found live 2026-07-20 on the first real Windows box test: (a) BuildCryptRemote
         was trimming the trailing slash off the bucket prefix — S3 has no real directories, so a
         brand-new/never-synced prefix with zero objects under it can't be told apart from "doesn't
         exist" without one, and rclone bisync's both-sides-same-type precondition check failed with
         "Source doesn't exist or is a directory and destination is a file" on every first sync
         attempt; now always appends exactly one trailing slash. (b) subst'd drives showed as
         "Local Disk" in Explorer with no way to tell Private from Public — added a
         DriveIcons\<Letter>\DefaultLabel registry write (SetDriveLabel) alongside every subst call,
         both the immediate current-session one and the persisted per-user logon script. (c) Stop()
         only disposed the sync timer — disabling the feature via the WebUI toggle left both drive
         letters mounted in Explorer AND the per-user logon task still registered (which would have
         re-mounted them again at the next logon); Stop() now also unsubst's both letters, clears
         their drive-label registry keys, and unregisters the logon task. 1.8.2 = the 1.8.1
         trailing-slash fix wasn't enough on its own — the REAL S3-compatible endpoint URL (which
         contains "://") broke rclone's connection-string grammar even when embedded/quoted inside
         the nested s3 spec, failing with "Custom endpoint `https` was not a valid URI" (truncated
         at the first colon). Confirmed live via a local rclone repro against the real R2 endpoint:
         moved endpoint/access_key_id/secret_access_key/region OUT of the connection string
         entirely and into RCLONE_S3_* environment variables on the rclone process instead (env
         vars have no delimiter grammar, so this class of bug can't recur) — the same repro then
         reached a real HTTP round-trip to R2. Connection string is now just
         `:crypt,remote=":s3:<bucket>/<prefix>/",password=<obscured>:`, nothing else nested. -->
    <!-- 1.8.3 = the per-user logon subst task visibly flashed a PowerShell console for 3-4s at
         every reboot/logon despite `-WindowStyle Hidden` — found live 2026-07-20, a known Windows
         11 regression where Windows Terminal (the default console host) doesn't reliably honor
         PowerShell's own -WindowStyle flag the way legacy conhost.exe did. Fixed by launching
         through a tiny VBScript wrapper (WScript.Shell.Run(cmd, 0, True)) instead of invoking
         powershell.exe directly from the scheduled task — wscript.exe has no console window at
         all, and its Run() window-style argument is a completely different, far more reliably
         honored code path. -->
    <!-- 1.8.4 = fixed a live EDR false-positive spam bug found 2026-07-20 on the two dev-owned
         test VMs: EdrWatcher.cs's IsOwnTooling() only recognized our own tooling as the DIRECT
         parent image. On a VM where OpenSSH's default exec shell is PowerShell, every legitimate
         Hop-2 call (BitLocker's 15-min status poller, AppLocker, session_powershell, etc.) spawns
         one extra hop deeper: sshd-session.exe -> powershell.exe -c "powershell ...
         -EncodedCommand ...". IsOwnTooling never saw through that wrapper, so every single
         legitimate call paged as a process_lineage/encoded-powershell-command finding, unbounded,
         forever (confirmed live: 15-min-interval pages for 6+ hours straight). Added
         IsOwnToolingLineage(), which walks up one more level through the existing in-memory
         lineage buffer (ProcessCache) only when the direct image is a generic PowerShell host — a
         real attacker's PowerShell process spawned by a non-own-tooling parent still alerts
         normally. Applied to all three rule call sites (process_lineage, create_remote_thread,
         lsass_access) for consistency. A second, independent bug in the same investigation (the
         bastion's signal_send() on every high-severity report had NO suppression at all,
         completely bypassing server.js's 30-min EDR_ALERT_SUPPRESS_MS window, which only guards a
         separate alert path) was fixed bastion-side in the support service — no agent change needed
         for that half. -->
    <!-- 1.8.5 = fixed a live remote-update-safety bug found 2026-07-20 while rolling out 1.8.4 to
         a dev-owned account-linked test VM: Program.cs's unattended-tunnel branch checked
         SessionEmbedded.IsConfigured (compile-time config baked into a specific exe by the
         bastion's per-session ad-hoc builder) BEFORE AccountLinkedSession.IsPreviouslyEnrolled (a
         disk-persisted, exe-independent fact about the device). remote-update-session.sh's remint
         step always rebuilds through the per-session ad-hoc builder, so reminting an
         already-account-linked device produced an exe that silently downgraded it to
         RunUnattendedAdHocTunnel() (Hop-1 admin tunnel only) on every future unattended-tunnel
         launch — the dashboard/device_status control channel died with no way back short of
         reinstalling the true account-linked build, and Hop-1 stayed healthy throughout so it was
         invisible from the admin side (confirmed live: bastion showed connected+correct version,
         SentinelMDM dashboard showed the device Offline on stale pre-restart data indefinitely).
         Swapped the check order — IsPreviouslyEnrolled now wins, matching MainForm.cs's own
         (already-correct) prioritization at its equivalent gate. Safe: RunUnattendedAccountLinkedSocket()
         already starts Hop-1 on demand via AccountLinkedSession.SessionRequested, a strict
         superset of the ad hoc path. -->
